WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:06.470
Film Courage:&nbsp; You had early success in your
screenwriting career as you had your first

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:08.690
screenplay optioned for a few thousand dollars
in the beginning?

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.269
Danny Strong:&nbsp; Yes.
Film Courage:&nbsp; And then you wrote for six

00:00:11.269 --> 00:00:13.369
years and you were able to sell&nbsp;RECOUNT&nbsp;to
HBO.&nbsp; So most people would have given up

00:00:13.369 --> 00:00:15.120
in that six year period.&nbsp; Especially now
when everybody wants things instantly.&nbsp; Why

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.299
didn’t you give up?
Danny Strong:&nbsp;&nbsp;That’s a great question.&nbsp;

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.060
I mean the early success wasn’t really success
[Laughs]…I would say.&nbsp; I wouldn’t really

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.779
call it early success.&nbsp; I don’t even view
it that way.&nbsp; I view it as six years of no

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:28.460
one wanting my stuff and it was some random
person who was very kind who was interested

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:35.120
in the script.&nbsp; But it wasn’t as if it
was all of the sudden “I made it and it’s

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.910
happening now!”&nbsp; So it really was a six,
seven year journey into selling my first project

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:44.520
which was recount and why I didn’t give
up I think is because I had something to say

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.503
and I loved writing these scripts and they
were important to me and I loved working on

00:00:47.503 --> 00:00:48.980
stories and it just was artistically, extremely
fulfilling where my acting career was very

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.649
frustrating.&nbsp; And I worked…I worked on
some terrific shows that I’m really proud

00:00:52.649 --> 00:00:57.329
of, but I wasn’t actually getting to act
very much.&nbsp; So even when you’re working…you

00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:01.359
know I would do episodes of&nbsp;Gilmore Girls&nbsp;and
I would do four episodes a season and I would

00:01:01.359 --> 00:01:08.110
work on it a day or two and that would be
one of my only jobs for six months.&nbsp; So it’s

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:14.900
really about seven days on set over a six
month period.&nbsp; So what am I doing the rest

00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:19.439
of the time?&nbsp; I’m auditioning and trying
to get new jobs but that’s an hour a day

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.079
maybe?
So really it was a creative outlet for me

00:01:23.079 --> 00:01:28.950
of taking my creative energy and putting it
into something where I can actually just go

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:34.020
do it.
Where as an actor, you have to be picked to

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.079
go do it and then you rarely get picked.&nbsp;
So I just found it fulfilling but I also was

00:01:39.079 --> 00:01:42.550
getting rejected left and right, script after
script and agents, managers, production companies,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.369
everyone didn’t want my work for six, seven
years.&nbsp; And I got to a point where I asked

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:53.880
myself this is not going well. What do you
want from this?&nbsp; What are you doing?&nbsp; And

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.810
I sort of asked myself would I (and it’s
one of the key questions in the movie&nbsp;REBEL

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:06.691
IN THE RYE)&nbsp; would I do this for the rest
of my life if I got nothing in return and

00:02:06.691 --> 00:02:10.690
I thought “Yeah…I would.”&nbsp; And I just
kept writing and then eventually, it wasn’t

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.580
as if six months later…crack the champagne!
It was maybe three years later that I sold

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.900
RECOUNT as a pitch to HBO and then just kind
of went on from there.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.330
But there was a certain peace in knowing that
it wasn’t about selling something or success

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:27.610
(even though I really wanted it.&nbsp; I wasn’t
a Kumbaya moment but there was a peace in

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.800
knowing that well whatever happens you just
keep writing.&nbsp; So I just kept writing and

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.250
that’s what I did.
Film Courage:&nbsp; You included that in two parts

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:43.180
of the movie [REBEL IN THE RYE] that premise
[keep writing even if you get nothing in return]

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.720
and that’s a real eye-opener that most people
may not be ready for.

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:49.990
Danny Strong:&nbsp;&nbsp;Yeah. And look what&nbsp;J.D.
Salinger&nbsp;did, right?&nbsp; He stopped publishing

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:55.420
at the height of his fame and then continued
writing for 45 years for nothing in return

00:02:55.420 --> 00:03:04.260
and he wrote every day and he just did it
because he just wanted to write and I think

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:11.700
to me that’s what makes this story a profound
story is the journey of the artist and he

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:17.160
goes from being a young many who all he wants
is success to being an older writer which

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.700
all he wants is to just create his art and
nothing else.&nbsp; And that’s the journey.&nbsp;

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.700
That’s the Aesop fable of this movie.
Film Courage: Would you say you still spend

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.780
300 days a year writing EMPIRE?
Danny Strong:&nbsp; No.&nbsp; I was the first two

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.890
seasons of&nbsp;EMPIRE. I was very active and
then with season three I switched to consulting

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:40.290
and made&nbsp;REBEL IN THE RYE.&nbsp; So now with
seasons three and four, I’m still the executive

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.430
producer on it, but I’m not nearly as hands
on as I was for the first two seasons.

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:48.490
Film Courage:&nbsp; You probably couldn’t have
written EMPIRE and REBEL IN THE RYE at the

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.760
same time?
Danny Strong:&nbsp; Well I think I wrote REBEL

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.770
IN THE RYE the first couple drafts before
EMPIRE happened.&nbsp; And actually EMPIRE happening

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:01.400
slowed my progress in getting REBEL IN THE
RYE made because EMPIRE got made.&nbsp; So I ended

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:09.170
up working on that.&nbsp; But I ended up directing
a lot of episodes of EMPIRE, so that was an

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.630
amazing experience for me to transition into
directing.&nbsp; It just couldn’t have been

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:21.800
a better way to really learn that craft because
you have to move so quickly and I have plenty

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.560
more to learn but it was a great start for
me.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:29.880
Film Courage:&nbsp; When you are writing…and
I understand you have this writing day where

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:36.760
you have your breakfast, get a lot of “housekeeping”
out of the way, but is it easier to write

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:39.860
in New York versus LA?&nbsp; Or the place doesn’t
really matter?

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:45.070
Danny Strong:&nbsp; Well I love New York City.&nbsp;
I love being there.&nbsp; I’m just so happy

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:51.140
there, so for me I can write anywhere and
that’s kind of one of the fun things about

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:55.930
writing is I can be anywhere in the world
and I can write. And it’s actually pretty

00:04:55.930 --> 00:04:59.320
cool to be in some random city some where
in some random coffee shop and you’re writing

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.060
and it’s like how often does someone have
a job that they can do anywhere in the world?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:07.352
I mean that’s a lot of fun!
And I’m perfectly productive in Los Angeles.&nbsp;

00:05:07.352 --> 00:05:11.450
I just love being in New York and it’s just
an exciting place to be.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:16.160
Film Courage:&nbsp; Can you relate to&nbsp;J.D. Salinger’s
process in terms of where once he crossed

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.350
over into meditation and all of that and he
needed to really barricade himself?&nbsp; Can

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.980
you relate to this?
Danny Strong:&nbsp;&nbsp;I definitely don’t barricade

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:29.450
myself at all.&nbsp; I actually write in public
places.&nbsp; I don’t like being in a quiet

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:35.710
room by myself.&nbsp; I have a certain amount
of ADD and being in a quiet room I zone out

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:39.210
and I’m less productive.&nbsp; So I actually
like having people around me.&nbsp; I have my

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:44.250
headset on, I listen to music. I write to
movie scores.&nbsp; So I’m just listening to

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:59.170
movie scores and people are around and there’s
an energy and I find it very helpful.&nbsp; And

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:11.790
it just helps me focus, but that’s just
me.&nbsp; So the isolation of what&nbsp; Mr. Salinger

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:19.950
did is kind of the opposite of how I work.
Film Courage:&nbsp; For new writers, I know they

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:32.270
are told to get feedback from other people.&nbsp;
I was recently reading&nbsp;Anne Lamott’s Bird

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:40.310
By Bird&nbsp;and she talked about writing conferences
and how they can be helpful, but at the same

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:47.440
time the slashing to the edits is also stabbing
the screenwriter a little bit.&nbsp; Can you talk

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:55.300
about the kindest way to give someone honest
feedback?&nbsp; So you are actually helping them,

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:01.490
but you are not wounding them so they won’t
go on [to write].

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:11.790
Danny Strong:&nbsp; Well I think that the best
way to give feedback and I very much rely

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:26.620
on feedback and I need notes from people and
it helps me see problems and it’s a very

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:33.790
important part of my process is getting feedback.
But I think to keep…I mean good feedback

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:39.360
is when it’s just about the script.&nbsp; It
doesn’t get personal.&nbsp; It doesn’t talk

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:51.400
about bigger ideas of whether you can do this
or not or you have talent or not. Where it’s

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:57.250
literally about how to make this script or
this story better in areas that are problematic.&nbsp;

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:05.040
I think it’s really important when someone
is getting feedback that they talk to a few

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:17.360
people.&nbsp; That they get feedback from maybe
three people.&nbsp; Because there could be feedback

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:32.900
that someone gives you that they are very
passionate about but they don’t know what

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:40.450
they’re talking about.&nbsp; It’s bad feedback
and they’re just not good at giving feedback.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:57.890
There’s a lot of people that aren’t good
at giving feedback, right?&nbsp; So you want a

00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:23.570
few opinions.&nbsp; And for me when I start hearing
from two or three people the same problem

00:09:23.570 --> 00:09:49.470
they’re having, well there’s probably
a problem there and there is probably something

00:09:49.470 --> 00:10:16.390
I need to address. And one of my favorite
parts of 

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:26.710
writing is when I take a problem and then
I fix it 

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:31.930
and then it’s good. That’s so exciting
because you have something that was bad that’s

00:10:31.930 --> 00:11:05.950
now good and it’s like you have a hole and
instead of a ditch, you have a statue there

00:11:05.950 --> 00:11:34.440
instead.&nbsp; So 

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:42.300
I can only get there by getting feedback from
people.

00:11:42.300 --> 00:11:58.540
Film Courage: &nbsp;When do you start writing
everyday?

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:18.550
Danny Strong:&nbsp;So I have basically the same
pattern when I’m writing. Sometimes if I’m

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:41.589
in production it will change. &nbsp;But if I’m
just in 

00:12:41.589 --> 00:14:04.010
a writing phase (which is my favorite 

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:29.089
to be honest with you 

00:14:29.089 --> 00:15:02.230
because 
I love my schedule as a writer) I wake up,

00:15:02.230 --> 00:16:06.850
I go somewhere, have breakfast and usually
I can go somewhere and write 

00:16:06.850 --> 00:17:27.529
and then I’ll open my computer…so now
it’s around 10:30

